I think I made a mistake earlier. When I was trying to convert the division into multiplication, I just multiplied the numerators and denominators together without considering the signs of the terms. But looking back at the original problem, I see that it's a division of fractions.

To convert it to multiplication, I need to invert the second fraction and change the division sign to multiplication. So the correct expression would be:

\(
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{x-1}{6} \div \frac{x^{2}-1}{9} \\
= & \frac{x-1}{6} \times \frac{9}{x^{2}-1} \\
= & \frac{x-1}{6} \times \frac{9}{(x+1)(x-1)}
\end{aligned}
\)

Now I can multiply the numerators and denominators together:

\(
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{(x-1) \times 9}{6 \times (x+1)(x-1)} \\
= & \frac{9 x-9}{6(x+1)(x-1)}
\end{aligned}
\)

I think the correct answer is D. \( \frac{9 x-9}{6(x+1)(x-1)} \), but I notice that it's not among the options. I'm not sure which one is correct.

